
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Last week Apple updated the <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/mac/accessories/mice-keyboards/keyboards">Magic Keyboard</a>, replacing text key labels with glyphs, which is kind of nice. I have a similar keyboard on my laptop, and I like it. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">However, I was hoping they would fix a different injustice that&#8217;s been around for a while. Specifically, you can buy the <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/product/mjm64ll/a/magic-keyboard-with-touch-id-and-numeric-keypad-for-mac-models-with-apple-silicon-usb-c-us-english-white-keys">extended keyboard</a> with black or white keycaps, but the <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/product/mjly4ll/a/magic-keyboard-with-touch-id-for-mac-models-with-apple-silicon-usb-c-us-english">standard Magic Keyboard</a> only comes with white keycaps. I find this baffling.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There are plenty of us picky users who want an Apple keyboard on our desk (with Touch ID) that is not 16 inches wide. And yet, still no dice.</p>
